 "beats": [
  {
   "beat": 1,
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 5.357,
   "text": "Every faucet in town needs about fifty P-S-I of push. So you put a giant pump at the water plant."
  },
  {
   "beat": 2,
   "start": 5.426,
   "end": 9.809,
   "text": "It works, until seven a.m. Everyone showers at once, and the pressure crashes."
  },
  {
   "beat": 3,
   "start": 9.843,
   "end": 16.278,
   "text": "So you buy a pump big enough for the morning rush. Now it sits idle most of the day, and when the power goes out, every faucet goes dry."
  },
  {
   "beat": 4,
   "start": 16.348,
   "end": 21.913,
   "text": "Here's the trick: water is heavy. A column one foot tall pushes down with almost half a P-S-I."
  },
  {
   "beat": 5,
   "start": 22.017,
   "end": 27.27,
   "text": "Stack it about a hundred and fifteen feet tall, and you get fifty P-S-I. No motor. Just gravity."
  },
  {
   "beat": 6,
   "start": 27.304,
   "end": 30.957,
   "text": "So you lift a tank higher than every house in town. That's a water tower."
  },
  {
   "beat": 7,
   "start": 31.061,
   "end": 34.737,
   "text": "Now a small pump can fill it slowly, all night, while everyone's asleep."
  },
  {
   "beat": 8,
   "start": 34.759,
   "end": 40.07,
   "text": "In the morning rush, the tank does the pushing. Power goes out? It keeps pushing, often for about a day."
  },
  {
   "beat": 9,
   "start": 40.139,
   "end": 48.093,
   "text": "And why is the tank so fat? Pressure depends on height, not width. A wide tank holds a ton of water, so the level barely drops, and your pressure stays steady."
  },
  {
   "beat": 10,
   "start": 48.116,
   "end": 51.803,
   "text": "The water at the bottom is kept in reserve, so firefighters always have some."
  },
  {
   "beat": 11,
   "start": 51.85,
   "end": 56.557,
   "text": "But wait. That fire hydrant on your street sits on the water main all winter. Why doesn't it freeze?"
  }
 ],
